{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And she called his name Shelah, and he was in Cheziv when she gave birth to him</b> — Rashi writes (Rashi on Genesis 38:5): \"I say that it is called Cheziv because she ceased giving birth, in the language of 'Will you be to me like a deceptive brook' (Jeremiah 15:18) — for if so, what does it come to teach us?\" And I do not know why the name of the place would be called on account of this, for there is nothing novel in that event — it is enough that she gave birth to three sons, and at the time of her giving birth it was not known whether she had ceased or would bear thereafter; only at the time of her death was the matter known. And there are those who say (the Radak and Maharam of Rothenburg) that it was their custom that the father would name the firstborn and the mother would name the second; therefore for the first it says \"and he called his name\" and for the second \"and she called,\" and for the third it says \"and she called\" because Judah was in Cheziv when she gave birth to him, and he was not there to give him a name. But there is no reason or sense in this. And the opinion of Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra) is that Scripture told where they were born, and said \"when she gave birth to him\" as though it said \"when she gave birth to them,\" for they were all born in one place. In my opinion, Shelah is of the language of something that ceases and deceives, as in \"Do not deceive me\" (II Kings 4:28), regarding which the Targum rendered \"Do not make your statement false concerning your maidservant\" — or perhaps it relates to the language of error, \"שָׁלוּ\" (he erred), for one who acts unwittingly deceives in his thought. And Scripture says that she called him Shelah after the name of the place, for he was in Cheziv when she gave birth to him, in the manner of \"and let it come to pass, the young woman\" (Genesis 24:14). And this is their statement in Bereshit Rabbah, as expounded:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "Rashi's comment and the verse from Jeremiah are rendered as Ramban quotes them; the phrase לא תשלה appears in II Kings 4:28, though Ramban cites it correctly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:35.275508086Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}